WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.060
With major blockbusters and tentpole franchises
taking up most of our attention these days,

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:09.280
it can be easy to lose track of all the great
movies sneaking under the radar — but these

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:11.960
2018 fantasy movies are well worth seeking
out.

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:13.500
Annihilation

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:18.250
Based on the first book in Jeff VanderMeer's
The Southern Reach trilogy, Annihilation marks

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:22.960
Alex Garland's second outing as a director
after his successful debut with 2015's Ex

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:25.340
Machina — and he’s delivered once again.

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:30.220
The story is told from the perspective of
Lena, an army vet and cellular biology professor.

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:34.310
She joins an intrepid band of women as they
enter "The Shimmer," a mysterious area in

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:38.520
the swamplands of Florida where some abnormal
activity has been taking place after a recent

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:39.280
meteor strike.

00:00:39.580 --> 00:00:44.170
Lena's primary goal is to find out what happened
to her husband Kane, the comatose lone survivor

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:46.080
of a doomed expedition into the Shimmer.

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:50.180
As the team pushes on into the heart of the
Shimmer, strange things begin to unfold and

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:54.300
evolve, as the group finds themselves fighting
against the environment, each other — and

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:55.300
even themselves.

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:57.960
“You’re saying we get out by going deeper
then.”

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:00.350
“Yeah, if you like.”

00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:01.080
“No.

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:02.220
I don’t like.”

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:06.890
Annihilation is a fascinating workout for
the imagination, simultaneously pushing the

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:09.380
viewer to freakish levels of stress and wonder.

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:11.080
I Kill Giants

00:01:12.170 --> 00:01:16.500
Using the imagination to escape the pain of
reality is a common theme in childhood stories.

00:01:16.510 --> 00:01:19.580
But it can be hard to capture in a movie — which
makes Anders Walter's I Kill Giants even more

00:01:19.580 --> 00:01:20.080
of a gem.

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:24.340
In his smashing feature directorial debut,
Walter has successfully brought Joe Kelly's

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:28.800
graphic novel to life, offering viewers a
mesmerizing tale of heartache and adventure.

00:01:29.020 --> 00:01:32.720
Barbara Thorson is a restless 12-year-old
girl living on Long Island, where she's an

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:36.740
outcast at school in spite of attempts made
by her school counselor to reach out to her.

00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:41.160
But rather than bury herself in video games
or books, she takes on a different hobby:

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:42.120
giant slaying.

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:46.350
The film beautifully weaves together childhood
sorrow and thrilling escapism, along with

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:48.580
an epic dose of fantastical action.

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:53.230
With solid directing, acting and CGI, this
all-around high-quality feature has a kind

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:57.320
of Pan's Labyrinth feel to it, and is sure
to appeal to the giant-slayer inside you.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:01:58.920
The Endless

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:03.780
Blockbusters can be fun and full of eye candy,
but there's always something extra special

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:07.400
about a well-made movie that didn't have access
to a gargantuan budget.

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:12.450
Case in point: Justin Benson and Aaron Moorhead's
The Endless, a not-quite-sequel to 2012’s

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:13.020
Resolution.

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:17.240
Benson and Moorhead directed, shot and starred
in The Endless, joining forces to deliver

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:21.700
a shocking fantasy-tinged story of cultish
fear to disturb even the most stalwart horror

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:22.200
fan.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:26.680
The film's storyline follows Justin and Aaron
Smith as they warily return to the death cult

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:27.360
they’d once escaped.

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:32.379
Once there, the seemingly unnatural peace
is slowly and masterfully broken down in a

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:35.959
series of revelations that expose a Lovecraftian
underbelly of horror.

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:37.720
“It shows us what it sees.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:42.520
There’s a powerful elegance to that.”

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:47.880
Tastefully terrorizing, this film blends elements
of fantasy, sci-fi and horror, all without

00:02:47.889 --> 00:02:52.069
relying on an inflated budget or A-list actors
— neither of which stopped The Endless from

00:02:52.069 --> 00:02:54.240
attracting near-universal critical acclaim.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:56.040
Psychokinesis

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:58.760
How do you think you gain telekinetic powers?

00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:02.160
Well, by drinking water from a mountain spring
hit by a meteor, of course.

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:06.620
At least that's what Train to Busan’s director
Sang-ho Yeon had in mind when he made one

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:09.640
of the most refreshingly unusual superhero
movies of the year.

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:14.379
Written and directed by Yeon, Psychokinesis
is a good reminder that superhero movies don't

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:16.080
always have to be about saving the world.

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:20.900
The story is inwardly focused, following the
origin of the unlikely hero Seok-heon, who

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:22.820
accidentally gains telekinetic powers.

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:27.320
It's all just in time to receive a distress
call from his estranged daughter Roo-mi, informing

00:03:27.329 --> 00:03:31.349
him that an aggressive, mob-run construction
company is threatening her trendy fried chicken

00:03:31.349 --> 00:03:32.300
restaurant in Seoul.

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:36.280
Launched into the action as he's still just
beginning to understand and control his own

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:40.740
abilities, Seok-heon comes across as a perfectly
illegitimate superhero, frustrated by his

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:43.300
own powers and often unable to think on his
feet.

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:47.200
Simple and refreshingly light, it's a superhero
movie of down-to-earth proportions — and

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:50.500
a welcome change of pace in a Marvel and DC-dominated
world.

00:03:57.280 --> 00:03:58.780
The Man Who Killed Don Quixote

00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:03.640
Before the turn of the millennium, director
Terry Gilliam set out to film an adaptation

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:07.199
of Miguel de Cervantes' classic 1615 novel
Don Quixote.

00:04:07.199 --> 00:04:10.670
His pursuit of that goal became the stuff
of Hollywood legend as Gilliam battled through

00:04:10.670 --> 00:04:15.680
a seemingly endless series of production delays,
cast turnovers, and legal and financial fiascos.

00:04:15.969 --> 00:04:20.580
It even inspired its own documentary, 2002's
Lost In La Mancha, which followed the futility

00:04:20.590 --> 00:04:24.020
of an early attempt that included Johnny Depp
cast as Sancho Panza.

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:28.509
But Gilliam's vision for his comically romanticized
film was set with gritty determination, and

00:04:28.509 --> 00:04:32.389
literally decades later the movie has finally
arrived in the form of The Man Who Killed

00:04:32.389 --> 00:04:33.240
Don Quixote.

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:37.400
The films plot progresses through an absurd
and fantastic series of events, with Adam

00:04:37.409 --> 00:04:41.810
Driver playing a simple yet perfect foil to
Jonathan Pryce's wide-eyed, ludicrously charming

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:44.680
shoemaker — who believes he is the real
Don Quixote.

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:48.580
It's a fun movie with a great story behind
it, which only serves to make it that much

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:50.030
more interesting to watch.

